building libstdoc
=================

Requirements
------------

  * CMake 3.20 or newer.  That is the first release that knows C++23,
    which the library implementation is written in.
  * GNU or Clang C and C++ compilers (the flags are GNU-style and are
    only used after the compiler has been identified).
  * A C++23 compiler whose standard library supports freestanding
    mode: the implementation includes "<climits>", "<cstdarg>",
    "<cstddef>" and "<cstdint>" with "-ffreestanding -fno-exceptions
    -fno-rtti".  libstdc++ provides that from GCC 13 on; libc++ and the
    MSVC standard library do not implement freestanding mode, and the
    configuration fails with a message that names what is missing
    instead of quietly building a hosted program.
  * Linux on aarch64 or on x86_64.  Any other operating system or
    architecture is rejected at configure time rather than silently
    mapped onto an implementation that does not match it: the build
    fails with a message that names what is missing.

No network access, no package manager and no third-party dependency
is needed at any point.


Quick start
-----------

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

Builds are out-of-source only: "build" is the conventional directory
name and is already ignored by Git.  When no build type is given, the
configure step selects RelWithDebInfo rather than leaving the build
with no optimisation flags at all; see below.


Build options
-------------

CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE
    RelWithDebInfo when the variable is not set at all or is set to
    the empty string.  That build type uses -O2 with -g, so a default
    build is optimised and still carries debug information.  Debug,
    Release and MinSizeRel are chosen explicitly:

        cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
        cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release

    The choice is recorded in the cache, so a later build in the same
    directory keeps it.  A multi-configuration generator such as Ninja
    Multi-Config or Visual Studio chooses the type with
    --config instead, and the default above does not apply to it.

STDOC_BUILD_TESTS
    ON by default.  Set to OFF to build only the library:

        cmake -S . -B build -DSTDOC_BUILD_TESTS=OFF

STDOC_HEAP_SIZE
    Size in bytes of the dynamic memory arena (4096 by default).  It
    reaches the allocator as a compile definition, so the configured
    value is the only one the library is built with; it reaches the
    test programs the same way, so none of them is built against a
    different value from the library it links.

    The smallest arena that can work is 64 bytes: every block carries a
    header of its own, and an arena that cannot hold one block and the
    payload of another can serve no program at all.  The configuration
    guard rejects anything below 64, and anything that is not a plain
    decimal number, when the project is configured rather than when it
    runs.

    cmake -S . -B build -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=1024

    The tests do not depend on the value: they ask the allocator what
    it has and shape each scenario to fit, so the same sources pass from
    64 bytes upwards, and a smaller heap only makes a leak show up
    sooner.


How the flags are chosen
------------------------

The library is compiled as freestanding C++23 and the tests are not,
for reasons that matter:

    library    -std=c++23 <C++ warning set> -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
               -fno-stack-protector -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti
               -fno-threadsafe-statics

    C tests    -std=c17 <C warning set> -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector

    C++ test   -std=c++17 <C++ warning set>

    link       -nostdlib -static

  * "-ffreestanding" is deliberately not used for the test programs:
    it makes clang treat main() as an ordinary function, mangles its
    name and removes the implicit "return 0", so the program would
    exit with whatever the last call left in the return register.
  * "-fno-builtin" stops a call from being rewritten into a compiler
    builtin that this library does not provide.
  * "-fno-stack-protector" removes the need for a failure handler
    symbol that does not exist here.
  * "-fno-exceptions", "-fno-rtti" and "-fno-threadsafe-statics" keep
    the C++ runtime out of the archive: no unwinder, no personality
    routine, no type information and no guard variables for
    function-local statics.  An object that would need dynamic
    initialization is rejected while the library is compiled - the
    stream objects and the heap arena are "constinit", which is
    exactly the promise the freestanding entry point needs, since it
    runs no constructors.
  * "-nostdlib -static" makes the library's own _start, its printf and
    its string helpers the only ones available at link time, which is
    the point of the whole exercise.  "-static" is also what keeps the
    result a plain non-PIE executable: both drivers already imply
    "-no-pie" for a static link, and passing it as well only makes
    Clang report it as an unused argument on every link.
  * The default RelWithDebInfo build type contributes "-O2 -g
    -DNDEBUG".  The library itself has no assert calls, so -DNDEBUG
    changes nothing in the archive; -O2 is what lets the block copies
    in io/string.cxx become wide loads and stores, and -g keeps a
    failure readable in a debugger.  Build with Debug for an
    unoptimised run and with Release when the debug information is not
    wanted.

The same warning set is used for the library and for the C tests, so a
test can never be sloppier than the code it exercises:

    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror
    -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
    -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes -Wcast-qual
    -Wundef -Wswitch-default -Wdouble-promotion -Wvla

The C++ sources use the same set without the two prototype options,
which only exist for C, and with four additions that keep a C++
implementation honest:

    -Wold-style-cast -Wnon-virtual-dtor -Woverloaded-virtual
    -Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant

Every one of them is understood by both GNU C++ and Clang, so no check
is made before they are passed.  Two test programs add "-Wno-format" to
the set for a reason that is written down where it applies; see
docs/testing.

The public header and the C test programs are built as C17 with
extensions off, and the implementation is built as C++23, also with
extensions off.  The C++ compatibility test is built as C++17 on
purpose: the archive is meant to be consumable from an older C++ as
well, and the test proves it.

A static library is never linked on its own, so the properties above
are checked on the archive itself: the archive_is_freestanding test
inspects every member for references to a C library or a C++ runtime,
for exported mangled names and for sections that run code before the
program.  See docs/testing.


Installation
------------

The default prefix is /usr/local, which normally needs the privileges
of the root user:

    cmake -S . -B build
    sudo cmake --install build

A per-user installation needs no privileges and puts everything below
the home directory:

    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cmake --install build

The prefix belongs on the configure line, not on the install line:
the paths written into libstdoc.pc are fixed when the project is
configured, so "cmake --install build --prefix ..." moves the files
but leaves the metadata pointing at the configured prefix.

Four things are installed: the archive, the two headers, a CMake
package and a pkg-config file.


Consuming the library
---------------------

With pkg-config:

    cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libstdoc)

A prefix outside the search path of pkg-config, such as the home
directory, is not looked in by default:

    PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/.local/lib/pkgconfig" \
        cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libstdoc)

With CMake:

    find_package(libstdoc REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(my_program PRIVATE libstdoc::stdoc)

pointing CMake at the prefix with -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<prefix>.  The
imported target carries the include directory with it.

The archive is linked the same way from C and from C++: it exports the
C interface, refers to nothing outside itself except the program's
main, and needs no C++ runtime, so a C program can link it with a C
driver and a C++ program needs no C++23 support of its own - the C++
compatibility test is built as C++17 to prove that.

Note that the library is freestanding by design: a program linked
without a C runtime needs the entry point that libstdoc provides, and
one linked with a normal C runtime gets its entry point from there
instead.

One detail of linking without a C runtime is worth knowing before it is
met the hard way, because the symptom - the program dies with SIGBUS
before main() has done anything - says nothing about the cause.  GNU ld
places the read-write segment at a file offset congruent to its virtual
address modulo the maximum page size of the target (64 KiB on aarch64,
4 KiB on x86_64), and it pads the file out to that offset only when it
has something to write there.  A static
executable with nothing file-backed in its read-write segment - no
global object with an initializer, nothing the linker has to write into
the image - therefore has that segment placed past the end of the file,
the kernel maps the page from there, and the first access to the .bss
that follows it is reported as SIGBUS.  One initialized object anywhere
in the program is enough to prevent it, and a program that uses
stdoc_stdout or stdoc_stderr already has several.  Linking with
"-z max-page-size=4096" avoids it as well, at the cost of executables
that a kernel with 64 KiB pages may refuse to load; the test suite
prefers the initialized object.  test/largewrite.c records the same
thing where it applies.


Continuous integration
----------------------

Two workflows build this library, each on a runner of the
architecture it tests, so nothing there is cross-compiled:
aarch64-linux-gnu-ubuntu-lts-26.04.yml runs on an aarch64 runner and
x86_64-linux-gnu-ubuntu-lts-26.04.yml on an x86_64 one, both on Ubuntu
26.04 LTS and under .github/workflows/.  Each checks three things on
every push and pull request:

  * the build and the test suite under GNU C and under Clang, with
    RelWithDebInfo for the ordinary rows and Debug for the
    smallest-arena row, at the default arena size and at the smallest
    one the configuration accepts, so the boundary the allocator
    documents is exercised as well as the ordinary case;
  * test/expected-format.txt regenerated from the host C library, which
    fails if the golden file in the repository no longer matches what a
    reference library prints, so it cannot drift into being a snapshot
    of libstdoc;
  * the configuration guard, which has to reject the arena sizes it
    documents as impossible.

The x86_64 workflow checks the architecture guard as well: a configure
for a processor this library does not implement - i686, arm, riscv64
or ppc64le - has to fail and name the processor it refused.

Every step the workflow runs can be run by hand with the commands in
this document and in docs/testing.


Porting
-------

The platform-specific pieces are chosen in exactly two places, and a
new operating system or architecture has to satisfy both:

  1. CMakeLists.txt decides what is supported.  It accepts Linux on
     aarch64 (aarch64, arm64, ARM64) and Linux on x86_64 (x86_64,
     amd64, AMD64) and fails with a clear message for anything else,
     so an unimplemented target can never silently pick an
     incompatible implementation.  The processor it selects also names
     the assembly files of the target.

  2. The implementation itself:
       assembly/start_<processor>.S    program entry point,
       assembly/syscall_<processor>.S  system-call trampoline,
       io/syscall_linux.cxx            system-call numbers, one block
                                       per architecture.

A port adds the files for its target, teaches the CMake guard about
them and adds the architecture's system-call numbers to
io/syscall_linux.cxx, keeping every operating-system-specific and
architecture-specific value out of the portable code in io/ and mem/.
The system-call layer is the only place where numbers and register
conventions appear.

Cross-compilation is not claimed to work: it needs a toolchain for
the target and a successful build before anyone can say it does.
